Z||000000||Ben84||10-09-2001||11:48 PM||||During my vacation in Nov., I plan on removing my Cat Converter (straight pipe) and installing a less restrictive muffler on my 84 2m4..... Do any one have any suggestions on a quality muffler manufacture or a particular muffler???? Just trying to produce a nice sounding but less restrictive Duke.... Thanks||||reg||6
Z||000001||pepper||10-10-2001||04:31 AM||||flowmaster would be a good one<BR>check out <A HREF="http://www.prostreet.nl" TARGET=_blank>www.prostreet.nl</A> ||||reg||10
Z||000002||fotofrank||10-10-2001||01:41 PM||||Try a muffler for a 2.8 V6, I did. It is less retrictive then the Duke muffler and you get dual exhaust to boot.Not only that but it is a direct fit. Sounds different too. Just removing the CAT changes the sound.<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||10
Z||000003||FieroMeister||10-11-2001||01:51 PM||||OK, I will admit to being just a bit of a 'tree hugger' but I would suggest that you leave the catalytic converter and drop the muffler.  In the '80s this is what Herb Adams did with all his stock V6 racers.  He said that the cat did not show any restriction to the exhaust system but the muffler did.  I have heard a few cars modified this way and I have to admit that they sound pretty good.  Louder than stock for sure, but the kind of louder that most of us like.  This way you get the lower restriction and you remain legal.  Herb's testing showed that, at that time, the sound levels with the muffler removed would be legal in all states.  Can't beat that as far as I'm concerned.  Just my opinion of course!<p>[This message has been edited by FieroMeister (edited 10-11-2001).]||||reg||3
